// Rep Max questions: Religious Studies (AQA 8062MA), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"grs-001","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":1,"q":"Which word describes the Christian belief that God is one being in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit?","o":["Incarnation","Trinity","Atonement","Ascension"],"a":1,"x":"The Trinity is the belief in one God in three persons. Incarnation is God becoming human, atonement is making amends, and the Ascension is Jesus returning to heaven.","w":["Incarnation is God becoming human, not God being three persons in one.","","Atonement is about Jesus' death mending the link with God, not God's nature.","The Ascension is an event, Jesus returning to heaven, not a belief about God's nature."]}
{"id":"grs-002","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":2,"q":"Why do some people say that evil and suffering are a problem for belief in the Christian God?","o":["An all-loving, all-powerful God would be expected to stop suffering","Christians believe God cannot see what happens on Earth","The Bible never mentions evil or suffering","Christians believe God took no part in creation"],"a":0,"x":"The problem of evil asks how an all-loving, all-powerful God can allow suffering. Christians respond in different ways, for example by pointing to free will.","w":["","Christians believe God is all-knowing, so this is not the problem raised.","The Bible talks about suffering a lot, for example Job and the crucifixion.","Christians believe God created the world, so this is not the problem raised."]}
{"id":"grs-003","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":3,"q":"Which belief about life after death is held by many Roman Catholics but rejected by most Protestant churches?","o":["Purgatory, a state of purification after death before heaven","Judgement by God after death","The resurrection of the body","Heaven as eternal life with God"],"a":0,"x":"Catholics teach that some souls are cleansed in purgatory before entering heaven. Most Protestants reject this, but both groups believe in judgement, resurrection and heaven.","w":["","Both Catholics and Protestants believe in judgement, so it doesn't divide them.","Both traditions believe in resurrection, so it doesn't divide them.","Both Catholics and Protestants believe in heaven, so it doesn't divide them."]}
{"id":"grs-004","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":1,"q":"What do Christians mean by the Ascension?","o":["Jesus rising from the dead on the third day","Jesus returning to heaven forty days after the resurrection","Jesus being baptised by John in the River Jordan","The Holy Spirit coming to the disciples"],"a":1,"x":"Christians believe that after the resurrection Jesus was taken up into heaven in front of his disciples (Acts 1). The Spirit coming at Pentecost is a separate event.","w":["This is the resurrection, which happened before the Ascension.","","This is Jesus' baptism, at the start of his ministry.","This is Pentecost, which comes after the Ascension."]}
{"id":"grs-005","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":2,"q":"What does the word 'atonement' mean in Christian teaching?","o":["God taking human form in Jesus","A priest announcing forgiveness in confession","Jesus' death repairing the broken relationship between God and humans","Jesus rising from the dead at Easter"],"a":2,"x":"Atonement means 'at-one-ment': Christians believe Jesus' death on the cross made up for human sin and restored people's relationship with God. God becoming human is the incarnation.","w":["This is the incarnation, not atonement.","This is absolution, which is different from atonement.","","This is the resurrection; atonement is about the effect of Jesus' death."]}
{"id":"grs-006","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement best matches the Protestant (especially Lutheran) view of how people are saved?","o":["Salvation is earned only by keeping the Ten Commandments","Salvation comes only through sacraments given by a priest","Salvation is God's free gift of grace, received through faith in Christ","All people are saved whatever they believe or do"],"a":2,"x":"Many Protestants teach justification by faith: salvation is God's free gift of grace, not earned by good works. Catholics also teach that salvation comes by grace, but stress faith shown in good works and the sacraments.","w":["Works can't earn salvation for Lutherans; it is by grace through faith.","Protestants say grace is received by faith, not only through a priest's sacraments.","","This is universalism; Lutherans teach salvation is received through faith in Christ."]}
{"id":"grs-007","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":1,"q":"Which Christian sacrament uses water to welcome a person into the Church?","o":["Eucharist","Marriage","Baptism","Anointing of the sick"],"a":2,"x":"Baptism uses water, poured or by full immersion, to show cleansing from sin and entry to the Church. The Eucharist uses bread and wine; anointing uses oil.","w":["The Eucharist uses bread and wine, not water.","Marriage uses vows and rings, not water.","","Anointing of the sick uses oil, not water."]}
{"id":"grs-008","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"A church service that follows a set order of prayers and readings is called:","o":["Liturgical worship","Non-liturgical worship","Charismatic worship","Private worship"],"a":0,"x":"Liturgical worship follows a fixed pattern, as in Catholic and Anglican services. Non-liturgical worship is freer, and charismatic worship stresses the Holy Spirit.","w":["","Non-liturgical worship is freer, without a fixed script.","Charismatic worship is spontaneous and focuses on the Holy Spirit.","Private worship is done alone, not a church service with a set order."]}
{"id":"grs-009","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"Many Catholics go on pilgrimage to Lourdes in France because they believe that:","o":["Jesus was crucified there","St Columba founded a monastery there in 563","the first Christian church was built there","Mary appeared to a girl called Bernadette there in 1858"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics believe Mary appeared to Bernadette Soubirous at Lourdes in 1858. Many pilgrims seek healing from its spring. Iona in Scotland is linked to St Columba.","w":["Jesus was crucified in Jerusalem, not Lourdes.","St Columba's monastery was on Iona in Scotland, not Lourdes.","Lourdes is known for Mary's appearances, not for the first church.",""]}
{"id":"grs-010","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a Christian charity that works to end poverty around the world?","o":["Greenpeace","Amnesty International","Christian Aid","Médecins Sans Frontières"],"a":2,"x":"Christian Aid is supported by many UK churches and works to tackle poverty overseas. Greenpeace, Amnesty and MSF are not Christian organisations.","w":["Greenpeace is an environmental group, not a Christian charity.","Amnesty campaigns for human rights and is not a Christian charity.","","MSF is a secular medical charity, not a Christian one."]}
{"id":"grs-011","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":1,"q":"What is evangelism?","o":["Helping refugees to settle in a new country","Praying quietly in a church building","Sharing the Christian message in order to bring others to faith","Making peace between two groups in conflict"],"a":2,"x":"Evangelism means telling others about Jesus and inviting them to become Christians. Helping the poor or making peace are different parts of the Church's work.","w":["Helping refugees is practical care, not sharing the faith.","Quiet prayer is worship, not telling others about Christ.","","Making peace is reconciliation, not evangelism."]}
{"id":"grs-012","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":3,"q":"Why do many Christians quote the parable of the sheep and the goats (Matthew 25) when supporting work to end poverty?","o":["Jesus says the poor will always be with us, so nothing can change","Jesus teaches that wealth is a sign of God's blessing","Jesus says charity should always be kept secret","Jesus says that helping people in need is the same as helping him"],"a":3,"x":"In Matthew 25 Jesus says that what people did for 'the least of these' they did for him. Christians use it to argue that they must feed and care for the poor.","w":["'The poor you will always have' is Matthew 26, and it isn't a reason to do nothing.","The parable isn't about wealth as blessing; it is about caring for the needy.","Secret giving is from the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 6), not this parable.",""]}
{"id":"grs-013","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":1,"q":"What does the Muslim belief in Tawhid mean?","o":["The prophethood of Muhammad","The oneness of Allah","The Day of Judgement","The existence of angels"],"a":1,"x":"Tawhid is the belief that Allah is one, with no partners or equals. It is the most important Muslim belief and the heart of the Shahadah.","w":["Confuses Tawhid with Risalah; Tawhid is about Allah, not Muhammad's role.","","Akhirah (the afterlife) is a different belief; Tawhid is Allah's oneness.","Angels (Malaikah) are a separate article of faith, not Tawhid."]}
{"id":"grs-014","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":2,"q":"Which statement best describes the belief in al-Qadr (predestination) held by many Muslims?","o":["Humans have no choice because every action is forced","Allah knows all that will happen, yet humans are free and accountable","Allah only learns what happens after it has happened","Allah decides who goes to heaven so actions do not matter"],"a":1,"x":"Many Muslims believe Allah's knowledge covers everything, but people still have free will and will be judged on their actions. Shi'a Muslims especially stress Allah's justice and human freedom.","w":["That is fatalism; Muslims believe people still choose and are judged on it.","","Allah's knowledge is complete, including the future; He does not learn later.","Actions matter because Allah judges them; heaven is not simply pre-picked."]}
{"id":"grs-015","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":3,"q":"Which of these is one of the five Roots of Religion (Usul ad-Din) in Shi'a Islam?","o":["Al-Qadr, predestination","Adalat, the justice of Allah","Al-Malaikah, belief in angels","Salah, daily prayer"],"a":1,"x":"The Shi'a Roots are Tawhid, Adalat, Nubuwwah, Imamah and Ma'ad. Predestination and angels belong to the Sunni articles of faith; Salah is a practice, not a Root.","w":["Al-Qadr is a Sunni article of faith, not one of the Shi'a Roots.","","Belief in angels is a Sunni article of faith, not a Shi'a Root.","Salah is a practice (Furu ad-Din), not a Root of Religion."]}
{"id":"grs-016","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":1,"q":"Muslims believe the Qur'an was revealed to Muhammad through which angel?","o":["Mika'il","Israfil","Azra'il","Jibril"],"a":3,"x":"Muslims believe Jibril (Gabriel) brought Allah's words to Muhammad over about 23 years. Mika'il, Israfil and Azra'il are other angels with different roles.","w":["Mika'il is linked with rain and provision, not bringing the Qur'an.","Israfil will blow the trumpet on the Last Day; he did not bring the Qur'an.","Azra'il is the angel of death, not the messenger of revelation.",""]}
{"id":"grs-017","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":1,"q":"Which holy book do Muslims believe was given to the prophet Isa (Jesus)?","o":["Injil","Tawrat","Zabur","Sahifah"],"a":0,"x":"Muslims believe Allah sent earlier books: Tawrat to Musa, Zabur to Dawud and Injil to Isa. Muslims believe the Qur'an is Allah's final, unaltered revelation.","w":["","Tawrat was given to Musa (Moses), not Isa.","Zabur was given to Dawud (David), not Isa.","Sahifah are the scrolls of Ibrahim, not the book given to Isa."]}
{"id":"grs-018","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":3,"q":"What do Shi'a Muslims believe about the Imams?","o":["They are elected by the Muslim community after each leader dies","They receive new revelation and write new holy books","They are chosen by Allah, starting with Ali, to guide Muslims after Muhammad","They are prophets who come after Muhammad"],"a":2,"x":"Shi'a Muslims believe Allah appointed Ali and his descendants as Imams to guide Muslims. Sunni Muslims have caliphs chosen by the community, and both groups say Muhammad was the last prophet.","w":["That describes Sunni caliphs; Shi'a believe Imams are appointed by Allah.","Imams guide and interpret, but they do not receive new revelation or books.","","Muhammad is the last prophet; Imams are leaders, not prophets."]}
{"id":"grs-019","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":1,"q":"Why do Muslims perform wudu before Salah?","o":["To mark the end of a fast","To make themselves ritually clean before praying","To show they can afford the Hajj","To prepare halal food"],"a":1,"x":"Wudu is the washing of hands, mouth, face, arms and feet before Salah. It makes a Muslim clean in body and mind to stand before Allah.","w":["A fast is ended at iftar with food or water, not with wudu.","","Wudu has nothing to do with Hajj; it is a ritual wash before prayer.","Preparing halal food is separate; wudu is washing the body before Salah."]}
{"id":"grs-020","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is one of the Shi'a Ten Obligatory Acts but is not one of the Five Pillars?","o":["Hajj","Sawm","Zakah","Khums"],"a":3,"x":"Khums is a tax of one fifth on surplus income, paid by Shi'a Muslims. Hajj, Sawm and Zakah are among the Five Pillars and also among the Ten Obligatory Acts.","w":["Hajj is a Pillar too, so it does not set the Shi'a list apart.","Sawm is a Pillar too, so it does not set the Shi'a list apart.","Zakah is a Pillar too, so it does not set the Shi'a list apart.",""]}
{"id":"grs-021","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":3,"q":"How does the Shahadah often differ among Shi'a Muslims compared with Sunni Muslims?","o":["Many Shi'a add a statement that Ali is the friend of Allah","They leave out the name of Muhammad","They say it only once in their lifetime","They replace it with the call to Salah"],"a":0,"x":"The Sunni Shahadah has two parts: no god but Allah, and Muhammad is his messenger. Many Shi'a add a third statement about Ali as the friend (wali) of Allah.","w":["","Shi'a still say Muhammad is Allah's messenger; they add to it, not remove.","Shahadah is said daily and in prayer, not just once in a lifetime.","The call to Salah (Adhan) is separate and does not replace the Shahadah."]}
{"id":"grs-022","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":1,"q":"In which month do Muslims fast from dawn to sunset for Sawm?","o":["Ramadan","Muharram","Shawwal","Dhul Hijjah"],"a":0,"x":"Sawm takes place in Ramadan, the ninth month, when the Qur'an was first revealed. Shawwal begins with Id-ul-Fitr, and Hajj takes place in Dhul Hijjah.","w":["","Muharram is the first month and includes Ashura; the fast is in Ramadan.","Shawwal comes after Ramadan and begins with Id-ul-Fitr.","Dhul Hijjah is the month of Hajj and Id-ul-Adha, not Sawm."]}
{"id":"grs-023","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"What do Shi'a Muslims remember on Ashura?","o":["Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son","The martyrdom of Husayn at Karbala","The first revelation of the Qur'an to Muhammad","The end of the month of fasting"],"a":1,"x":"Ashura marks the death of Husayn, grandson of Muhammad, at Karbala in 680 CE. Many Sunni Muslims also fast on Ashura, remembering Allah saving Musa. Id-ul-Adha remembers Ibrahim, and Id-ul-Fitr ends Ramadan.","w":["That is Id-ul-Adha, not Ashura.","","The first revelation is remembered in Ramadan (Laylat al-Qadr), not on Ashura.","That is Id-ul-Fitr, which ends Ramadan, not Ashura."]}
{"id":"grs-024","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"On Hajj, what is the name for walking seven times around the Ka'bah?","o":["Sa'i","Wuquf","Ramy","Tawaf"],"a":3,"x":"Tawaf is circling the Ka'bah seven times. Sa'i is walking between Safa and Marwah, wuquf is standing at Arafat, and ramy is throwing stones at the pillars.","w":["Sa'i is walking between Safa and Marwah, not circling the Ka'bah.","Wuquf is standing in prayer on the plain of Arafat, not circling the Ka'bah.","Ramy is throwing stones at the pillars in Mina, not circling the Ka'bah.",""]}
{"id":"grs-025","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":1,"q":"Why does the Roman Catholic Church teach that marriage cannot normally be ended by divorce?","o":["Jesus never spoke about marriage","It is only a legal contract with no religious meaning","Divorce is allowed but only for men","Marriage is a sacrament, a lifelong covenant made before God"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics believe marriage is a sacrament that lasts for life. The Church does not recognise divorce, though it can grant an annulment, which says a valid marriage never existed.","w":["Jesus did teach on marriage, saying what God has joined together must not be separated.","Catholics see marriage as far more than a legal contract; it is a sacred sacrament.","Catholic teaching on divorce is the same for men and women; it is not one-sided.",""]}
{"id":"grs-026","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":2,"q":"What is the usual Muslim teaching about divorce?","o":["It is forbidden in all circumstances","It is encouraged whenever a couple argue","It is allowed as a last resort but strongly discouraged","It is allowed only for men, who need no reason"],"a":2,"x":"In Islam marriage is a contract and divorce is permitted, for example by talaq or khula, but Muslims are urged to try reconciliation first. Both husbands and wives have ways to end a marriage.","w":["Divorce is permitted in Islam, so 'forbidden in all circumstances' is too strong.","Islam urges reconciliation first, so arguments alone are no reason to divorce.","","Wives can also seek divorce, for example by khula, and reasons and reconciliation matter."]}
{"id":"grs-027","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":2,"q":"Which would a humanist be most likely to use to judge whether a sexual relationship is right?","o":["Whether a priest has blessed it","Whether it is likely to produce children","Whether it follows the Ten Commandments","Whether it is consensual and harms no one"],"a":3,"x":"Humanists base morality on reason, human welfare and consent rather than on religious rules. Many therefore accept sex outside marriage if all involved agree and nobody is harmed.","w":["Humanists don't rely on religious authority, so a priest's blessing isn't the test.","Humanists don't judge sex by whether it produces children; contraception is accepted.","The Ten Commandments are religious rules; humanists base morality on reason and welfare.",""]}
{"id":"grs-028","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":1,"q":"In Christian teaching, which is a main purpose of the family?","o":["To bring up children to know and love God","To give fathers power over everyone else","To make sure children inherit money","To keep members apart from other communities"],"a":0,"x":"Christians see the family as the place where children are raised in love and taught the faith, and as a source of stability for society.","w":["","Christians see the family as built on love and respect, not on fathers having power.","Inheritance isn't the Christian purpose of family; raising children in faith and love is.","Christians want families to be part of the wider community and church, not cut off."]}
{"id":"grs-029","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":2,"q":"Which Bible teaching do many Christians use to support equality between men and women?","o":["Wives should submit to their husbands (Ephesians 5)","Women should keep silent in the churches (1 Corinthians 14)","God created humans in his image, male and female (Genesis 1:27)","Adam was formed first, then Eve (1 Timothy 2)"],"a":2,"x":"Genesis 1:27 says both men and women are made in God's image. Some Christians use the other passages to argue for different roles, though others read them differently.","w":["Ephesians 5 is often used to argue for different roles, not equality.","1 Corinthians 14 is often used to limit women's roles, not to support equality.","","1 Timothy 2 is often used to argue for male leadership, not equality."]}
{"id":"grs-030","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement reflects a common Muslim view of men and women?","o":["Women may not own property or earn money","Equal in worth before Allah, though many see family roles as different","Men and women must have identical roles in the family","Only men are responsible to Allah for their actions"],"a":1,"x":"Muslims teach that men and women are equal before Allah. Many see roles as complementary, with the husband providing, but Islam gives women rights to own property and to earn.","w":["Islam gives women the right to own property and earn money.","","Many Muslims see family roles as complementary, not identical.","Men and women are both individually accountable to Allah for their actions."]}
{"id":"grs-031","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":1,"q":"Which scientific theory says the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago from a tiny, hot, dense point?","o":["Evolution by natural selection","Plate tectonics","The Big Bang theory","The Steady State theory"],"a":2,"x":"The Big Bang theory says the universe expanded from an extremely hot, dense state. Evolution explains how living things change, and Steady State is an older rival theory.","w":["Evolution explains how living things change over time, not how the universe began.","Plate tectonics is about movement of Earth's crust, not the origin of the universe.","","Steady State says the universe had no beginning, so it is a rival to the Big Bang."]}
{"id":"grs-032","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":2,"q":"What does the Muslim idea of khalifah mean for how Muslims treat the environment?","o":["Humans own the Earth and may use it however they like","Nature does not matter because only the afterlife matters","Humans are Allah's trustees on Earth and must look after it","Only animals, not plants, must be protected"],"a":2,"x":"A khalifah is a steward or trustee. Muslims believe Allah created the world and made humans responsible for caring for it, and they will answer to Allah for how they treated it.","w":["Khalifah means trustee, not owner, so humans can't use the Earth however they like.","Muslims believe how we treat the world matters, as we answer to Allah for it.","","Khalifah covers all of creation, including plants, animals and resources."]}
{"id":"grs-033","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":2,"q":"Christians who understand 'dominion' (Genesis 1:28) as stewardship would most likely say that humans should:","o":["Use animals and nature in any way that benefits humans","Care for creation on God's behalf and use resources responsibly","Never use animals or land for any purpose","Leave nature alone because God will look after it"],"a":1,"x":"Stewardship means looking after creation as God's trustees. Some Christians read dominion as the right to rule over nature, but many stress responsibility and care.","w":["That is the 'rule over nature' reading of dominion, not the stewardship reading.","","Stewardship allows responsible use; it doesn't ban all use of animals or land.","Stewards must actively care for creation; God has given humans that responsibility."]}
{"id":"grs-034","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":1,"q":"What does the 'sanctity of life' mean?","o":["Life is only valuable if it is of good quality","Life is holy and is a gift from God","Life belongs to the person who lives it","Life has no purpose beyond the present"],"a":1,"x":"The sanctity of life is the religious belief that human life is sacred because God created it. It is often used to argue against abortion and euthanasia.","w":["That is a quality of life view, which values life only in some conditions.","","That is a personal autonomy view, not the religious idea that life belongs to God.","Sanctity of life says life has great value because it is holy, not that it lacks purpose."]}
{"id":"grs-035","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement about Christian views on abortion is accurate?","o":["Roman Catholic: acceptable if the mother chooses it","Roman Catholic: allowed freely in the first 12 weeks","Church of England: always a sin, with no exceptions","Church of England: usually wrong, but may be a lesser evil in some cases"],"a":3,"x":"Catholics teach that life begins at conception and deliberate abortion is always wrong. The Church of England strongly opposes abortion but accepts that in strictly limited cases it may be morally preferable to any other choice.","w":["Catholics teach that deliberate abortion is wrong, whatever the mother chooses.","Catholics oppose abortion at any stage, as they believe life begins at conception.","The Church of England accepts that abortion may be a lesser evil in limited cases.",""]}
{"id":"grs-036","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":2,"q":"Which is a typical humanist argument in support of voluntary euthanasia?","o":["People should be free to choose a dignified death to avoid unbearable suffering","God alone has the right to end life","Suffering always makes a person stronger","Life must be preserved at all costs"],"a":0,"x":"Humanists do not believe in a God who controls life and death. Many stress quality of life and personal choice, with safeguards, while religious believers often stress the sanctity of life.","w":["","This is a religious argument against euthanasia; humanists don't believe in God.","Humanists don't claim suffering is good; they stress reducing unnecessary suffering.","This is the sanctity of life view used against euthanasia, not humanist support for it."]}
{"id":"grs-037","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":1,"q":"What do pacifists believe?","o":["War is justified only if a religious leader declares it","Violence is acceptable if there is a just cause","War and violence are always wrong and disputes should be settled peacefully","Terrorism can be justified in extreme cases"],"a":2,"x":"Pacifists reject all war and violence. Just war theory allows war in some conditions, and holy war is fought for a religious cause. Quakers are well-known Christian pacifists.","w":["Mixes up pacifism with holy war; pacifists reject war whoever declares it.","That is just war thinking; pacifists say violence is never acceptable.","","Terrorism is violence, which pacifists reject in every case."]}
{"id":"grs-038","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is one of the conditions of a just war in Christian teaching?","o":["It must be a last resort after peaceful options have failed","It must be fought to gain land and resources","It must punish the enemy as harshly as possible","It must destroy the enemy's whole population"],"a":0,"x":"Just war conditions include just cause, right authority, right intention, last resort, proportionality and a reasonable chance of success. Greed or revenge are not just causes.","w":["","Greed is not a just cause; war for land or resources is aggression.","Revenge breaks proportionality; just war limits harm to what is needed.","Wiping out a population is hugely disproportionate and targets civilians."]}
{"id":"grs-039","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":3,"q":"Which statement describes the Muslim idea of 'lesser jihad'?","o":["The inner struggle to resist temptation and be a better Muslim","A pilgrimage to Makkah in the month of Dhul Hijjah","Armed struggle in defence of Islam or against oppression, with proper authority","Any war fought to spread Islam by force"],"a":2,"x":"Lesser jihad is armed struggle with strict conditions, such as defence of faith or fighting oppression. The inner struggle is the greater jihad. Many Muslims reject the idea that it means forcing belief.","w":["That is the greater jihad, the inner struggle, not the lesser one.","That describes Hajj, a pilgrimage, which is not jihad as struggle.","","Common myth; lesser jihad is defensive with strict rules, not forced conversion."]}
{"id":"grs-040","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":1,"q":"What does 'nuclear deterrence' mean?","o":["Getting rid of all nuclear weapons","Using nuclear weapons first to win quickly","Agreeing not to test nuclear weapons","Having nuclear weapons to discourage other countries from attacking"],"a":3,"x":"Deterrence is the idea that fear of massive retaliation stops enemies attacking. Getting rid of nuclear weapons is called disarmament.","w":["That is disarmament, the opposite of keeping weapons to deter.","First use is aggression; deterrence relies on the threat of retaliation.","That describes a test ban treaty, not deterrence.",""]}
{"id":"grs-041","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":2,"q":"Which Archbishop chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission, set up after apartheid?","o":["Oscar Romero","Justin Welby","Michael Ramsey","Desmond Tutu"],"a":3,"x":"Desmond Tutu, a Christian archbishop, chaired the commission set up in 1995. It encouraged people to tell the truth about abuses and to seek forgiveness and reconciliation.","w":["Romero was a Salvadoran archbishop killed in 1980, not linked to South Africa.","Welby became Archbishop of Canterbury in 2013, long after the commission.","Ramsey was Archbishop of Canterbury in the 1960s-70s, long before the commission.",""]}
{"id":"grs-042","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":2,"q":"Why do many Christians say nuclear weapons could never meet the just war conditions?","o":["They would kill huge numbers of civilians, which is not proportionate","They are too cheap to be a last resort","They can only be used by a legitimate government","They are not mentioned in the Bible so are banned"],"a":0,"x":"Just war theory requires proportionality and protection of civilians. Christians who oppose nuclear weapons argue that their effects are indiscriminate and far out of proportion.","w":["","Cost has nothing to do with last resort; that means peaceful options come first.","Legitimate authority is a just war condition, so it is not a reason against.","Not being in the Bible doesn't ban them; the argument is about just war principles."]}
{"id":"grs-043","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":1,"q":"What is a hate crime?","o":["Any crime committed in anger","A crime motivated by prejudice against a person's race, religion or similar","A crime committed by a group of people","A crime committed against a religious leader"],"a":1,"x":"A hate crime is committed because of hostility towards a person's race, religion, disability, sexual orientation or similar. Christians and Muslims believe all people are created by God, so all deserve respect.","w":["Anger alone is not enough; a hate crime needs prejudice against a group trait.","","Group crime isn't the point; a hate crime is defined by its prejudiced motive.","The victim needn't be a religious leader; any prejudice-based target counts."]}
{"id":"grs-044","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":2,"q":"Which teaching do Christians often use to support obeying the law?","o":["Romans 13: be subject to the governing authorities","Matthew 5: blessed are the peacemakers","Genesis 1: humans are made in God's image","Luke 15: the parable of the lost sheep"],"a":0,"x":"Romans 13 says governing authorities have been set up by God, so Christians should obey them. Some Christians, such as Martin Luther King, say unjust laws may be broken.","w":["","This is about peacemaking, not obeying the law.","This is about human dignity, not obeying the authorities.","This parable is about God seeking sinners, not about obeying laws."]}
{"id":"grs-045","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":2,"q":"A Muslim teaching on murder, often quoted from Surah 5:32, says that killing one innocent person is like:","o":["Missing one of the daily prayers","Breaking a promise to Allah","Stealing from a neighbour","Killing the whole of humanity"],"a":3,"x":"Surah 5:32 says whoever kills an innocent life is as if he had killed all people. Muslims use it to show the great value Islam places on human life.","w":["Missing prayer isn't the comparison; the verse likens murder to killing all people.","Far too mild; the verse compares murder to killing the whole of humanity.","Stealing is a lesser wrong; the verse compares murder to killing everyone.",""]}
{"id":"grs-046","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":1,"q":"Which aim of punishment is about putting people off committing crime through fear of the consequences?","o":["Reformation","Deterrence","Retribution","Protection"],"a":1,"x":"Deterrence aims to stop offenders and others from committing crime. Reformation aims to change offenders, retribution is getting 'payback', and protection keeps society safe.","w":["Reformation aims to change the offender, not frighten people off.","","Retribution is payback for the crime, not putting others off.","Protection keeps the public safe, e.g. by imprisonment, not fear of consequences."]}
{"id":"grs-047","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":2,"q":"Which Quaker is remembered for campaigning to improve conditions for women prisoners in the 1800s?","o":["Josephine Butler","Florence Nightingale","Mary Seacole","Elizabeth Fry"],"a":3,"x":"Elizabeth Fry, a Quaker, visited Newgate prison and worked to improve conditions for women. Her work is an example of Christians trying to reform offenders.","w":["Butler campaigned against trafficking of women, not prison conditions, and wasn't a Quaker.","Nightingale was a nurse, not a prison reformer.","Seacole nursed soldiers in the Crimean War; she was not a prison reformer.",""]}
{"id":"grs-048","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":3,"q":"What is the Roman Catholic Church's current teaching on the death penalty?","o":["It is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person","It is required for all murder","It is acceptable whenever the state decides","It is allowed only for theft"],"a":0,"x":"In 2018 Pope Francis changed the Catechism to say the death penalty is inadmissible. Some other Christians and Muslims accept it for the most serious crimes.","w":["","Catholic teaching does not require execution; it now opposes it.","The Church doesn't leave this to the state; it calls the death penalty inadmissible.","The Church now rejects the death penalty for every crime, theft included."]}
{"id":"grs-049","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":1,"q":"Christians believe God is all-powerful. Which word describes this part of God's nature?","o":["Omniscient","Omnibenevolent","Omnipotent","Immanent"],"a":2,"x":"Omnipotent means all-powerful. Omniscient means all-knowing, omnibenevolent means all-loving, and immanent means present in the world. Mixing up the 'omni' words is the common trap.","w":["Omniscient means all-knowing, not all-powerful, so this mixes up two 'omni' words.","Omnibenevolent means all-loving or all-good, not all-powerful.","","Immanent means God is present in the world, which is not about power."]}
{"id":"grs-050","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":2,"q":"One Christian says Genesis 1 is a symbolic story. It shows that God made everything, and it does not need to match science. Which describes this view?","o":["Genesis is a literal, factual record of six 24-hour days","Genesis gives a true message about God as creator, in symbolic form","Genesis shows that God did not create the universe at all","Genesis must be rejected because it disagrees with science"],"a":1,"x":"Many Christians read Genesis as symbolic truth: God is the creator, but the story is not a science report. Literal readers take the six days as fact. Do not confuse a non-literal reading with rejecting the Bible.","w":["This is the literal view held by some Christians, the opposite of what this person says.","","This person still believes God is creator and only reads the story as symbolic.","Non-literal readers still value Genesis for its message rather than rejecting it."]}
{"id":"grs-051","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1a","lv":3,"q":"Many Christians say much suffering comes from human free will. Which example would this answer explain LEAST well?","o":["A tsunami that kills thousands of people","A robber attacking a shopkeeper","A driver ignoring a red light and causing a crash","A bully posting cruel messages online"],"a":0,"x":"Free will explains moral evil, which comes from human choices. A tsunami is natural evil, with no human choice behind it, so free will does not explain it. Mixing up moral and natural evil is the trap.","w":["","An attack is a human choice, so free will explains it as moral evil.","Ignoring a red light is a human choice, so free will can explain it.","Cruel messages are chosen by a person, so free will explains this moral evil."]}
{"id":"grs-052","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":1,"q":"Why is the resurrection so important to Christians?","o":["It proves Jesus was only a great human teacher","It shows that Christians must keep all the Old Testament laws","It means sin no longer exists in the world","It shows Jesus defeated death and gives hope of eternal life"],"a":3,"x":"Christians believe that by rising from the dead Jesus overcame death. This gives believers hope of life after death. The trap is thinking it removes sin from the world. Christians believe it offers forgiveness, not that sin stops.","w":["Christians believe the resurrection shows Jesus is the Son of God, not just a human teacher.","The resurrection is about Jesus defeating death, not about keeping every Old Testament law.","Christians believe sin still exists; the resurrection offers forgiveness and new life, not the end of sin.",""]}
{"id":"grs-053","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":2,"q":"Many Christians believe the Holy Spirit has a part in salvation. What do they believe the Spirit does?","o":["Gives people strength and guidance to follow Jesus' teaching","Takes the place of Jesus' death as the way to be forgiven","Was born as a human being in Bethlehem","Gives people a place in heaven whatever they do"],"a":0,"x":"Many Christians believe the Spirit lives in believers, guiding and strengthening them to live as Jesus taught. The Spirit does not replace Jesus' sacrifice. Becoming human was the Son's incarnation, not the Spirit's.","w":["","Christians believe forgiveness comes through Jesus' death; the Spirit helps believers live their faith but does not replace it.","This mixes up the Spirit with the incarnation; Christians believe it was the Son who became human.","This confuses the Spirit's role with universalism; Christians do not teach the Spirit gives heaven automatically."]}
{"id":"grs-054","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement best explains why many Christians believe humans need Jesus to save them?","o":["Humans can earn forgiveness by good deeds, and Jesus only gave an example","Humans inherit a sinful nature from the Fall, so cannot mend their relationship with God alone","Humans were made without free will, so God caused them to sin","Only people who have not been baptised are affected by sin"],"a":1,"x":"Christians believe in original sin: since Adam and Eve's disobedience, all humans have a tendency to sin. They cannot put things right alone, so Jesus' sacrifice is needed. The trap is thinking good deeds alone are enough.","w":["Christians believe good deeds alone cannot repair the relationship with God; Jesus' death did this, not just his example.","","Christians teach humans have free will and chose to disobey; God is not said to cause sin.","This confuses sin with baptism; Christians believe all humans are affected by sin."]}
{"id":"grs-055","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":1,"q":"Many Christians keep Advent. What is Advent?","o":["The 40 days of fasting and prayer before Easter","The weeks of preparation before Christmas","The Midnight Mass service held on Christmas Eve","The day when the visit of the wise men is remembered"],"a":1,"x":"Advent is the season of preparation before Christmas, starting four Sundays before it. Many churches light an Advent wreath. The trap is mixing it up with Lent, which comes before Easter.","w":["This describes Lent, the time of preparation before Easter, not Advent.","","Midnight Mass is one Christmas service, not the whole season of preparation called Advent.","The wise men are remembered at Epiphany on 6 January, after Christmas, not in Advent."]}
{"id":"grs-056","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"A Baptist church baptises teenagers who ask for it, but does not baptise babies. Which reason would a Baptist give?","o":["Babies cannot be forgiven by God until they are teenagers","Full immersion in water would be too dangerous for a baby","Baptism is not in the Bible, so it is only a church custom","Baptism should follow a person's own choice to follow Jesus"],"a":3,"x":"Baptists practise believer's baptism. They teach that a person should choose faith for themselves before being baptised. The trap is thinking the reason is about safety or about forgiveness for babies.","w":["Baptists do not teach that babies are unforgiven; their reason is about personal choice, not forgiveness.","Safety is not the reason. Baptists wait because faith must be a personal decision.","Baptism is in the Bible; Jesus was baptised and Baptists follow his example.",""]}
{"id":"grs-057","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2a","lv":2,"q":"Which row correctly matches each day to what Christians remember at Easter?","o":["Maundy Thursday: Last Supper. Good Friday: crucifixion. Easter Sunday: resurrection","Maundy Thursday: crucifixion. Good Friday: Last Supper. Easter Sunday: resurrection","Maundy Thursday: Last Supper. Good Friday: resurrection. Easter Sunday: crucifixion","Palm Sunday: Last Supper. Good Friday: crucifixion. Easter Sunday: resurrection"],"a":0,"x":"Christians remember the Last Supper on Maundy Thursday, the crucifixion on Good Friday and the resurrection on Easter Sunday. Palm Sunday marks Jesus entering Jerusalem. The trap is swapping the order of events.","w":["","This swaps the first two days. The Last Supper came before the crucifixion.","This swaps Friday and Sunday. Jesus died on Good Friday and rose on Easter Sunday.","Palm Sunday remembers Jesus entering Jerusalem. The Last Supper is remembered on Maundy Thursday."]}
{"id":"grs-058","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":1,"q":"Street Pastors are Christians who work in many UK towns. What do they do?","o":["Run Sunday schools for children in church halls","Patrol town centres at night to help vulnerable people","Collect food for people who cannot afford to eat","Train new priests to serve in the Church"],"a":1,"x":"Street Pastors walk through town centres at night. They listen to people, give out water and flip-flops, and help those in trouble. The trap is confusing them with food banks, which are a different local project.","w":["Sunday schools teach children about faith and are not the work of Street Pastors.","","Collecting food describes a food bank such as those run with the Trussell Trust, not Street Pastors.","Training priests is done by the Church's colleges, not by Street Pastors."]}
{"id":"grs-059","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":2,"q":"The Corrymeela Community in Northern Ireland brings together Catholics and Protestants to build trust. Which type of Church work is this?","o":["Evangelism, because it aims to convert people to Christianity","Relief of poverty, because it gives money to the poor","Missionary work, because it sends people to other countries","Reconciliation, because it helps people on opposite sides to make peace"],"a":3,"x":"Reconciliation means bringing people together after conflict. Corrymeela does this between divided communities. The trap is thinking that any Christian group is doing evangelism, which is about sharing the faith.","w":["Corrymeela aims at peace, not conversion, so this mixes up evangelism with reconciliation.","Corrymeela's aim is peace rather than giving money, so this confuses reconciliation with poverty work.","Corrymeela works within Northern Ireland, so this confuses its peace work with overseas mission.",""]}
{"id":"grs-060","s":"rs","t":"3.1.2.2b","lv":3,"q":"Which teaching would a Christian most directly use to explain why churches in the UK should help Christians who are persecuted in another country?","o":["\"If one part of the body suffers, every part suffers with it.\"","\"Go and make disciples of all nations.\"","\"Blessed are the peacemakers.\"","\"Give to Caesar what belongs to Caesar.\""],"a":0,"x":"Paul teaches that the Church is one body (1 Corinthians 12). Christians say that all believers share each other's suffering. The trap is choosing the Great Commission, which is about mission, not support for the persecuted.","w":["","The Great Commission is about spreading the faith, so this confuses mission with caring for suffering believers.","This teaching is about making peace between people, not the Church being one body.","This teaching is about paying taxes to the state and says nothing about persecuted believers."]}
{"id":"grs-061","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":1,"q":"Muslims believe an angel will blow a trumpet to signal the start of the Day of Judgement. Which angel is this?","o":["Jibril","Mikail","Israfil","Izra'il"],"a":2,"x":"Muslims believe Israfil will blow the trumpet on the Day of Judgement. Trap: Jibril brought revelation, Mikail is linked with mercy and sending rain, and Izra'il is the angel of death.","w":["Jibril brought Allah's revelation to the prophets, including Muhammad, not the trumpet blast.","Mikail is linked with mercy and providing rain and food, not with blowing the trumpet.","","Izra'il is the angel of death, so this mixes up death with the start of Judgement Day."]}
{"id":"grs-062","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":2,"q":"Why does belief in Akhirah (life after death) often change how a Muslim lives?","o":["Allah will judge their actions, so they try to follow the Qur'an","Allah has already chosen who enters heaven, so actions do not matter","Only the five daily prayers will be judged, not other actions","Everyone enters Jannah, so there is nothing to worry about"],"a":0,"x":"Muslims believe every person is accountable to Allah and will be judged on their deeds. This encourages them to live by the Qur'an. Trap: confusing al-Qadr with fatalism.","w":["","This is a fatalist reading of al-Qadr, but Muslims believe people have free will and are judged on their deeds.","This wrongly narrows accountability, since Muslims believe judgement covers all actions and intentions, not just prayer.","This ignores accountability, as Muslims believe Allah judges people and both Jannah and Jahannam are real."]}
{"id":"grs-063","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1a","lv":3,"q":"Shi'a Muslims stress Adalat (the justice of Allah). Which argument links Adalat to human free will?","o":["Allah is just, so He never punishes anyone for any reason","Allah is just, so every person will be rewarded in Jannah","Allah is just, so He cannot know what people will choose to do","Allah is just, so He would not punish acts He forced people to do"],"a":3,"x":"Many Shi'a Muslims argue that punishing forced actions would be unjust, so people must be free to choose and be accountable. Trap: thinking justice means no punishment or no knowledge of the future.","w":["Justice does not mean no punishment, as Muslims believe Allah judges and punishes wrongdoing fairly.","Justice means fair reward and punishment, not that everyone is rewarded whatever they have done.","Muslims believe Allah knows everything, so free will does not mean Allah lacks knowledge of the future.",""]}
{"id":"grs-064","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":1,"q":"Muslims call Muhammad the 'Seal of the Prophets'. What do they mean by this?","o":["He was the first prophet Allah sent to humanity","He was greater than every other prophet and the only one given a book","He was the last prophet, so no prophet comes after him","He sealed the older holy books so no one could read them"],"a":2,"x":"Muslims believe Muhammad was the final prophet and that Allah's message is complete. 'Seal' means the end of the line. The trap is thinking it means first, or the only prophet with scripture.","w":["Adam is seen as the first prophet; 'seal' means last, not first.","Muslims honour all prophets, and others such as Musa and Dawud also received books.","","'Seal' refers to the end of prophethood, not to hiding or closing the earlier books."]}
{"id":"grs-065","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":2,"q":"Why do Muslims treat the Qur'an as having more authority than the Tawrat, Zabur and Injil?","o":["The earlier books were changed over time; the Qur'an is Allah's unchanged word","The earlier books never came from Allah at all","Muhammad wrote the Qur'an in his own words","The earlier books were only ever given to angels"],"a":0,"x":"Muslims believe the earlier books came from Allah but were altered or lost. They believe the Qur'an is Allah's final, preserved revelation in Arabic. The trap is thinking Muslims reject the earlier books as never divine.","w":["","Muslims believe the earlier books did come from Allah but were later altered or lost.","Muslims believe the Qur'an is Allah's own words revealed through Jibril, not Muhammad's writing.","Muslims believe the earlier books were revealed to prophets such as Musa, Dawud and Isa, not angels."]}
{"id":"grs-066","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.1b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement best describes the authority Shi'a Muslims give to the Imams in relation to the Qur'an?","o":["Imams may change Qur'anic teachings when times change","Imams are only political leaders with no spiritual role","Imams are worshipped alongside Allah as divine beings","Imams are guided by Allah to explain the Qur'an and the faith correctly"],"a":3,"x":"Shi'a Muslims believe Imams are appointed and guided by Allah to interpret the Qur'an and lead Muslims. They do not replace or change it. The trap is treating Imams as prophets or as divine.","w":["Imams interpret the Qur'an; Shi'a Muslims do not believe they can change or override it.","Shi'a Muslims see Imams as spiritual guides as well as leaders, not only political rulers.","Worship belongs to Allah alone; Shi'a Muslims honour Imams but do not worship them.",""]}
{"id":"grs-067","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":1,"q":"In which direction do Muslims face when they perform Salah?","o":["Towards the mosque in Madinah","Towards Jerusalem","Towards the Kaaba in Makkah","Towards the rising sun"],"a":2,"x":"Muslims face the qiblah, the direction of the Kaaba in Makkah, wherever they are in the world. This unites Muslims in worship. Madinah and Jerusalem are important places but are not the qiblah.","w":["Madinah is a holy city, but confusing an important place with the qiblah is the slip.","Early Muslims briefly faced Jerusalem before the qiblah was changed to Makkah, so this is out of date.","","Prayer direction is not linked to the sun, because the qiblah is fixed as the Kaaba."]}
{"id":"grs-068","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about Salah is true for many Shi'a Muslims compared with Sunni Muslims?","o":["They often combine the five daily prayers into three times of day","They only pray once a week on Fridays","They pray ten times a day, one for each Obligatory Act","They do not have to face the Kaaba when they pray"],"a":0,"x":"Salah is an Obligatory Act for Shi'a as well as a Pillar for Sunni. Many Shi'a join prayers together, so they pray at three times of day. The five prayers and the qiblah still matter.","w":["","Salah is still daily for Shi'a, so this confuses it with Jummah, the Friday congregational prayer.","The Ten Obligatory Acts are not ten prayers, because Salah is just one of the ten acts.","Shi'a also face the Kaaba in Makkah, as the qiblah is shared by all Muslims."]}
{"id":"grs-069","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2a","lv":3,"q":"A Shi'a Muslim tries to turn away from, and keep distance from, those who oppose Allah. Which of the Ten Obligatory Acts is this?","o":["Tawalla","Amr-bil-Maroof","Khums","Tabarra"],"a":3,"x":"Tabarra means disassociating from the enemies of Allah and his friends. Tawalla is its opposite: showing love and loyalty to Allah, the Prophet and the Imams. Do not mix up the pair.","w":["Tawalla is love and loyalty towards Allah, the Prophet and the Imams, so this mixes up the opposite pair.","Amr-bil-Maroof is encouraging good actions, not distancing from those who oppose Allah.","Khums is a tax of one fifth on surplus income, which has nothing to do with disassociation.",""]}
{"id":"grs-070","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"A Sunni Muslim has £4,000 in savings above the minimum amount. They pay Zakah at the usual rate of 2.5%. How much do they give?","o":["£10","£100","£400","£800"],"a":1,"x":"Zakah is 2.5% of eligible savings each year. 2.5% of £4,000 is £100. The trap is using a different rate, such as 10% or the 20% rate of Khums paid by Shi'a Muslims.","w":["This is 0.25%, a decimal slip; 2.5% of £4,000 is £100.","","This uses 10%, not 2.5%; Zakah is a fixed 2.5% of savings.","This uses 20%, which is the Shi'a Khums rate, not the Zakah rate."]}
{"id":"grs-071","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":1,"q":"At Id-ul-Adha, many Muslims sacrifice an animal. Which describes the traditional way the meat is shared?","o":["All given to the poor, with none kept by the family","All eaten by the family on the day","Sold to raise money for the mosque","Shared between the family, friends and the poor"],"a":3,"x":"Many Muslims divide the meat into three parts: for the family, for friends and neighbours, and for the poor. This remembers Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son and shows generosity. It is not all kept or all sold.","w":["The meat is traditionally split in three, so the family keeps some; giving everything away is a slip.","The meat is shared out; keeping it all for the family ignores the duty to give to the poor.","The meat is not sold for the mosque; it is shared as food for others.",""]}
{"id":"grs-072","s":"rs","t":"3.1.5.2b","lv":2,"q":"A Muslim student refuses to cheat in an exam because of their faith. Which idea does this best show?","o":["Greater jihad, the inner struggle to live as Allah wants","Lesser jihad, because it is a small, everyday act","Lesser jihad, the struggle to defend Islam from attack","Zakah, giving up something valuable for Allah"],"a":0,"x":"Greater jihad is the inner struggle against temptation and sin. Resisting cheating is an example. Lesser jihad is the outer struggle to defend Islam. The trap is thinking 'lesser' means 'smaller'.","w":["","'Lesser' does not mean small acts; lesser jihad is the outer struggle to defend the faith, and this is inner.","Lesser jihad means defending Islam, often in a just war; resisting cheating is not that.","Zakah is giving 2.5% of savings to the needy, not resisting temptation."]}
{"id":"grs-073","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":1,"q":"In Christian teaching about marriage, what does the word 'adultery' mean?","o":["Sex between two people who are both unmarried","Living together as a couple without being married","Sex between a married person and someone not their spouse","Ending a marriage legally through a civil court"],"a":2,"x":"Adultery is sex between a married person and someone other than their spouse. Christians see it as breaking the marriage vows and the commandment 'You shall not commit adultery'. The trap is mixing it up with sex before marriage.","w":["That describes sex before marriage, not adultery, which always involves at least one married person.","That is cohabitation, which is a different issue from adultery.","","That describes divorce, the legal ending of a marriage, not unfaithfulness within one."]}
{"id":"grs-074","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":2,"q":"What does the Roman Catholic Church mean by an annulment?","o":["A declaration that a valid marriage never existed in the first place","The Church ending a valid marriage after a long separation","A separation where the couple stay married but may marry others","Permission to remarry in church after any civil divorce"],"a":0,"x":"An annulment says that a marriage was never valid in the first place, for example because there was no true consent. It does not end a valid marriage. The trap is thinking it is just a Catholic version of divorce.","w":["","Catholics believe a valid marriage lasts for life, so this confuses annulment with divorce.","Separated Catholics are still married and cannot marry others, so this is not an annulment.","A civil divorce alone never lets a Catholic remarry in church; the first marriage must be declared invalid."]}
{"id":"grs-075","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1a","lv":3,"q":"The Church of England teaches that marriage is for life. A divorced person asks to marry again in a Church of England church. Which statement is correct?","o":["It is always refused, because the Church does not allow remarriage","The vicar must agree, because civil divorce makes the person free to marry","It is allowed only if the first husband or wife has died","It may be allowed, but the vicar can refuse after considering the circumstances"],"a":3,"x":"The Church of England still teaches that marriage is for life, but since 2002 it allows remarriage in church in some cases. The priest decides and can refuse on grounds of conscience. The traps are 'never' and 'automatic'.","w":["This confuses the Church of England with the Catholic Church; since 2002 it allows remarriage in some cases.","No vicar is forced to agree, because priests may refuse on grounds of conscience.","Divorced people whose former spouse is still alive can sometimes remarry in church, not only widowed people.",""]}
{"id":"grs-076","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":1,"q":"Which statement matches the official teaching of the Roman Catholic Church about men and women?","o":["Women can be priests, but only the Pope must be male","Men and women are equal in dignity, but only men can be priests","Women can be priests if their local community agrees","Women are less important than men in God's eyes"],"a":1,"x":"Catholics teach that men and women have equal dignity, as both are made in God's image. Only men can be ordained priests, because Jesus chose twelve male apostles. Equal worth is not the same as identical roles.","w":["The Catholic Church does not ordain women as priests at all, so this is wrong.","","Catholic teaching on ordination is set by the whole Church, not decided by local communities.","This confuses different roles with different worth, as Catholics teach men and women are equal in dignity."]}
{"id":"grs-077","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":2,"q":"A Christian quotes Galatians 3:28, which says that in Christ there is no \"male and female\". What point is she most likely making?","o":["Men and women must do exactly the same jobs at home and in church","Husbands should have authority over their wives, as Christ does","Men and women have equal value and standing as followers of Jesus","There are no real differences of any kind between men and women"],"a":2,"x":"Many Christians use this verse to show men and women are equally valued in Christ. The trap is thinking equal means identical. The verse is about equal standing, not about having the same roles.","w":["This confuses equality with identical roles, as the verse is about equal standing in Christ, not jobs.","The verse says all are one in Christ and does not say husbands have authority over wives.","","This overreads the verse, which is about equal standing in Christ, not denying all differences."]}
{"id":"grs-078","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.1b","lv":3,"q":"Aisha is a Muslim wife who earns more than her husband. Which view would many Muslims hold about her wages?","o":["She must hand them over for her husband to control and spend","She must give up her job, as a wife's place is only at home","She must now pay all the family's costs, as she earns more","They are hers to keep, and her husband must still provide for the family"],"a":3,"x":"Many Muslims teach that a woman owns her earnings and need not spend them on the family. The husband is still expected to provide. The trap is thinking her higher pay removes his duty or gives him her money.","w":["Islam gives a wife the right to own and control her own money, so her husband does not take it.","Islam does not forbid women from working, and many Muslim women have careers.","Many Muslims teach the husband has the duty to provide, and her higher pay does not move it to her.",""]}
{"id":"grs-079","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":1,"q":"Some Christians are called creationists. Which belief do they hold about how the universe began?","o":["The Big Bang happened by chance and God was not involved","The Genesis account is literally true: God made the universe in six days","Genesis is only a story and has no meaning for Christians","God made the universe and then had no further part in it"],"a":1,"x":"Creationists take Genesis 1 literally. They believe God created the universe and everything in it in six days. The trap is thinking all Christians read Genesis this way, as many read it as symbolic.","w":["Creationists believe God caused creation, so they reject the idea that it happened by chance without God.","","Creationists read Genesis as literally true, not as a meaningless story.","This describes a God who stays out of the world, but creationists believe God is involved in creation."]}
{"id":"grs-080","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":2,"q":"Which statement best describes a common Muslim teaching about how humans should treat animals?","o":["Animals have no value, so humans may treat them as they like","Muslims must never eat meat because it harms animals","Animals are worshipped by Muslims because they have souls like humans","Animals are Allah's creation: they may be eaten but must be treated kindly"],"a":3,"x":"Muslims believe animals are part of Allah's creation and humans are his khalifah (trustees). Animals may be used for food and work, but cruelty is forbidden. The trap is thinking 'use' means 'no limits'.","w":["Muslims believe animals are Allah's creation and humans are trustees, so cruelty is forbidden.","Islam allows Muslims to eat halal meat, so they are not required to be vegetarian.","Muslims worship only Allah; animals are valued as his creation but never worshipped.",""]}
{"id":"grs-081","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2a","lv":3,"q":"A Christian says: 'Genesis tells us why the universe exists. Science tells us how it began.' What does this view suggest about religion and science?","o":["They answer different questions, so a person can accept both","Genesis is a scientific account that replaces the Big Bang","Science disproves God, so a Christian must reject it","Genesis and science give the same details of how it happened"],"a":0,"x":"This view says religion and science do different jobs. Genesis gives the purpose and meaning, with God as creator, while science explains the process, such as the Big Bang. The trap is assuming the two must be rivals.","w":["","The speaker says science explains how the universe began, so Genesis does not replace the Big Bang.","The speaker accepts science as explaining how, so they do not see it as disproving God.","The speaker says Genesis explains why, not how, so it does not give the same scientific details."]}
{"id":"grs-082","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":1,"q":"Scientists explain that humans developed from earlier species over millions of years. What is this scientific theory called?","o":["The Big Bang theory","Creationism","Evolution by natural selection","The sanctity of life"],"a":2,"x":"Evolution by natural selection says species change slowly over time, and humans developed from earlier species. The trap is the Big Bang, which is about how the universe began, not how human life developed.","w":["The Big Bang theory explains how the universe began, not how humans developed from earlier species.","Creationism is the religious belief that God made humans, not a scientific theory.","","The sanctity of life is a religious belief that life is holy, not a scientific theory."]}
{"id":"grs-083","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":2,"q":"A man is in a coma and cannot say what he wants. His family and doctors agree to switch off his life support. Which term describes this kind of euthanasia?","o":["Non-voluntary euthanasia","Voluntary euthanasia","Involuntary euthanasia","Palliative care"],"a":0,"x":"Non-voluntary euthanasia is when the person cannot give consent and others decide. Voluntary needs the patient's own request. Involuntary means against the person's wishes. Mixing up non-voluntary and involuntary is a common trap.","w":["","Voluntary euthanasia needs the patient to ask for it, but this man cannot express his wishes.","Involuntary euthanasia is when the person wants to live but is killed anyway; that is not stated here.","Palliative care aims to ease pain and symptoms, not to end life."]}
{"id":"grs-084","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.2b","lv":3,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the Abortion Act 1967, as later amended, in Great Britain?","o":["Abortion is allowed up to 24 weeks only if the woman's life is in danger","Abortion is allowed up to 28 weeks if the father gives consent","Abortion is allowed up to 12 weeks with no doctor's agreement","Abortion is generally allowed up to 24 weeks with two doctors' agreement"],"a":3,"x":"The usual limit is 24 weeks, and two doctors must agree that legal grounds are met, such as risk to the woman's health. Later abortions are allowed only in serious cases. The father's consent is not required.","w":["The grounds are wider than danger to life; they include risk to physical or mental health.","The limit was lowered to 24 weeks in 1990, and the father's consent is not required.","The law requires doctors' agreement, and the usual limit is 24 weeks, not 12.",""]}
{"id":"grs-085","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":1,"q":"What does 'reconciliation' mean in the study of peace and conflict?","o":["Punishing those who caused harm until they say sorry","Bringing people in conflict back together and rebuilding their relationship","Agreeing to stop fighting while staying enemies","Paying money to the victims of a conflict"],"a":1,"x":"Reconciliation means ending hostility and restoring a broken relationship. It goes further than a ceasefire. Christians and Muslims both teach that forgiveness is often needed first. Compensation or punishment alone does not mean people are reconciled.","w":["Confuses reconciliation with punishment; reconciliation aims to restore the relationship, not to make someone suffer.","","A ceasefire only stops the fighting; reconciliation means the people involved are no longer enemies.","Describes compensation (reparation), which may help but does not by itself restore the relationship."]}
{"id":"grs-086","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":2,"q":"An elected government sends its army into a neighbouring country over a dispute. It has not tried talks or sanctions first. Which just war condition is most clearly broken?","o":["Right authority","Proportionality","Reasonable chance of success","Last resort"],"a":3,"x":"Just war theory says war must be a last resort, used only after peaceful options have failed. An elected government can have right authority. The question gives no sign of excessive force or likely failure.","w":["An elected government normally has right authority; the problem here is the order of its actions.","Nothing in the question mentions excessive force, so proportionality is not the clearest breach.","Nothing says the war is likely to fail, so this is not the condition being broken.",""]}
{"id":"grs-087","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4a","lv":3,"q":"A Christian supports the just war theory instead of pacifism. Which reason would they most likely give?","o":["Love for your neighbour can mean using force as a last resort to protect the innocent","Jesus taught 'turn the other cheek', so Christians must never fight back","The Bible says 'an eye for an eye', so revenge is always right","War is a good first way to settle any dispute between countries"],"a":0,"x":"Just war supporters argue that loving your neighbour can mean defending innocent people, as long as the war meets strict conditions. 'Turn the other cheek' is a pacifist argument. 'An eye for an eye' does not justify unlimited revenge.","w":["","This is a common pacifist argument, so it would not support just war.","Misreads 'an eye for an eye'; just war theory limits harm and rejects revenge as a motive.","Just war theory says war must be a last resort, not a first choice."]}
{"id":"grs-088","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":1,"q":"Which statement best describes one way religion can be a cause of conflict in the world today?","o":["All religions teach that war is the best way to spread their beliefs","Groups can clash over differing beliefs or over control of holy places","Religious leaders alone have the power to start wars between countries","Religion is the only cause of conflict in the modern world"],"a":1,"x":"Religion can add to conflict when groups disagree over beliefs or claim the same holy sites. It is rarely the only cause. Politics, land and resources matter too. Most religions also teach peace.","w":["Most religions teach peace and set limits on war, so this overgeneralises from extreme cases.","","Governments and political leaders start wars. Religious leaders may influence them but do not alone control them.","Conflicts usually have many causes, such as politics, land and wealth. 'Only cause' is too strong."]}
{"id":"grs-089","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":2,"q":"A Catholic parish raises money to give food and shelter to families fleeing a war. Which charity, set up by the Catholic bishops of England and Wales, could it give to?","o":["Oxfam","Islamic Relief","Médecins Sans Frontières","CAFOD"],"a":3,"x":"CAFOD is the Catholic Agency for Overseas Development. It helps victims of war and disaster. The trap is picking another well-known aid charity that is not Catholic. Many Christians give because they believe they should love their neighbour.","w":["Oxfam is a large charity but is not a religious one set up by Catholic bishops.","Islamic Relief is a Muslim charity, not a Catholic one.","Médecins Sans Frontières is a secular medical charity, not a Catholic agency.",""]}
{"id":"grs-090","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.4b","lv":3,"q":"Many Muslims reject a terrorist group's claim that its attacks are 'lesser jihad'. Which is the strongest reason based on Islamic teaching?","o":["Lesser jihad must be defensive and must not deliberately target innocent civilians","Jihad only ever means a personal inner struggle, never physical defence","Lesser jihad is only allowed against other Muslims","Any individual may declare lesser jihad whenever they feel angry"],"a":0,"x":"Lesser jihad is the physical struggle in defence of Islam, but it has strict rules. It must be defensive, called by a legitimate authority, and must protect civilians. The trap is thinking jihad can never be physical.","w":["","Greater jihad is the inner struggle, but lesser jihad is physical defence, so 'never physical' is wrong.","Lesser jihad is about defending Islam and the oppressed from attack, not a rule limiting fighting to fellow Muslims.","Legitimate authority is needed. A private individual cannot declare it, and anger is not a just cause."]}
{"id":"grs-091","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":1,"q":"A man steals bread because he cannot afford to feed his hungry children. Which cause of crime does this best show?","o":["Greed","Peer pressure","Poverty","Addiction"],"a":2,"x":"The man steals out of need, not for extra wealth. Poverty is a common cause of crime. The trap is greed, which means wanting more than you need. Many religious believers still say theft is wrong but show compassion.","w":["Greed means wanting more than you need; here the theft is to meet a basic need.","Peer pressure needs friends or a group pushing him; none is mentioned.","","Addiction means a craving for a substance or habit; none is described."]}
{"id":"grs-092","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":2,"q":"In the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5:21-22), Jesus repeats 'You shall not murder'. What does he add?","o":["Being angry with someone makes a person liable to judgement","Killing is allowed if it is done in self-defence","Only killing in secret counts as murder","Murder can only be forgiven by the victim's family"],"a":0,"x":"Jesus teaches that anger towards another person is also serious and liable to judgement. Christians use this to say wrong attitudes matter, not only wrong actions. The trap is thinking only the act of killing is condemned.","w":["","Jesus does not mention self-defence here; he talks about anger, not exceptions to the commandment.","Secrecy is not mentioned; Jesus widens the teaching to include anger.","The passage does not say this; forgiveness is taught elsewhere and is not limited to the family."]}
{"id":"grs-093","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5a","lv":3,"q":"A Muslim says a thief is still guilty even if Shaytan tempted him. Which Muslim belief best supports this view?","o":["Allah forces people to commit each crime","Shaytan has total control over human actions","Prayer wipes out the punishment for any crime","Humans have free will and will be judged for it"],"a":3,"x":"Muslims believe Allah gave humans free will. Shaytan can tempt but cannot force, so people are responsible for their choices and will be judged. The trap is blaming Shaytan completely and removing personal responsibility.","w":["Muslims believe Allah gave free will; this view would remove responsibility for crime.","Shaytan only tempts; he cannot control choices, so the thief remains responsible.","Prayer does not cancel accountability; sincere repentance is needed, and people are still judged.",""]}
{"id":"grs-094","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":1,"q":"The Old Testament says 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21). Which aim of punishment does this teaching fit best?","o":["Reformation","Deterrence","Retribution","Protection"],"a":2,"x":"'Eye for eye' means the punishment matches the harm done. That is retribution, or 'payback'. Do not mix it up with deterrence, which puts others off, or reformation, which changes the offender.","w":["Reformation is about changing the offender, but 'eye for eye' is about matching the harm done.","Deterrence is about putting people off crime, whereas this quote is about fair payback.","","Protection is about keeping the public safe, but this quote is about payback matching the harm."]}
{"id":"grs-095","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":2,"q":"Quakers are Christians who oppose the death penalty. Which reason do they give?","o":["There is 'that of God' in everyone, so all life is sacred and people can change","Only the victim's family has the right to decide a killer's punishment","Punishment should only be payback, and prison does this better than execution","Jesus taught that all courts and laws should be abolished"],"a":0,"x":"Quakers believe there is 'that of God' in every person. So every life is sacred and offenders can change. The trap is thinking they reject all punishment or care only about payback.","w":["","Quakers do not teach that victims' families decide punishments; their view comes from the sacredness of life.","Quakers stress reformation, not payback, so this confuses their view with retribution.","Jesus did not teach this, and Quakers accept that society needs courts and laws."]}
{"id":"grs-096","s":"rs","t":"3.2.1.5b","lv":3,"q":"Many Muslims accept the death penalty for murder under Shari'ah. Which statement about forgiveness in such a case matches Muslim teaching?","o":["The family must demand execution, because forgiving a killer is forbidden","Forgiveness is allowed only if the killer first converts to Islam","Only the judge may forgive, since the victim's family has no say","The family may forgive or accept compensation, and Allah rewards forgiveness"],"a":3,"x":"The victim's family may forgive or accept compensation instead of execution. Qur'an 42:40 says those who forgive and make peace are rewarded by Allah. The trap is thinking support for the death penalty rules out forgiveness.","w":["Forgiving is not forbidden; the Qur'an praises it, so execution is not compulsory.","Muslim teaching sets no condition that the killer must convert before being forgiven.","The victim's family does have a say, including the right to forgive or accept compensation.",""]}
